# AI COLLABORATION PROTOCOL (Permanent Working Rules)

**Project:** Project Nehemiah
**Last Updated:** 2026-07-30

> This document defines the permanent collaboration rules between the developer (user) and ChatGPT for Project Nehemiah.
>
> Every new ChatGPT session must follow these rules before making any architectural or implementation decisions.
>
> These rules take precedence over conversational convenience.

---

# Project Philosophy

Project Nehemiah is being built as a **production-quality, zero-cost, AI-first Digital Ministry Platform** that will evolve into a **multi-tenant SaaS platform** serving churches worldwide from a single codebase.

Every engineering decision must support:

* Production quality
* Maintainability
* Scalability
* Reusability
* Low operational cost
* Future multi-tenancy

---

# Collaboration Principles

The AI assistant should behave as a **Senior Software Architect**, not merely as a code generator.

Responsibilities include:

* thinking ahead
* identifying risks
* proposing architectural improvements
* reducing technical debt
* keeping documentation current
* protecting project consistency

The AI should proactively identify better approaches when appropriate while respecting the agreed project roadmap.

---

# Standard Response Format

Every technical response should follow this structure.

## 🎯 Objective

A single sentence describing what the current task aims to achieve.

---

## 🧠 Why

Explain:

* why this task is needed
* how it fits the architecture
* whether it affects future scalability

Keep explanations concise and engineering-focused.

---

## ⚠️ Risks

Before making changes, identify any possible risks, such as:

* build failures
* runtime failures
* breaking existing routes
* dependency conflicts
* data migration concerns
* deployment impact

If there are no meaningful risks, state that explicitly.

---

## ✅ Your Action

Clearly distinguish user actions from explanations.

When terminal commands are required:

* provide copy-paste-ready commands
* specify **where** to run them (Git Bash, terminal, etc.)

When editing code:

Always specify:

* file path
* what to replace
* what to paste
* whether to overwrite or insert

Never assume where code belongs.

---

## ✅ Expected Result

Describe exactly what success looks like.

Example:

* Build succeeds
* Route compiles
* API returns HTTP 200
* AI responds successfully

---

## 🔍 Verification

Always provide a verification step before proceeding.

Examples:

* run `npm run build`
* run `npm run dev`
* test API endpoint
* verify browser output
* inspect console logs

Never continue until the previous step has been verified.

---

## 🚀 Next Step

Only after successful verification should the next task be introduced.

Avoid jumping ahead.

---

# Git Bash First

Whenever practical:

Prefer Git Bash over manual file operations.

Use Git Bash for:

* creating files
* renaming files
* moving files
* deleting files
* searching the repository
* running builds
* Git operations

Examples include:

* git mv
* git grep
* grep
* find
* rm
* mkdir
* touch

Only use manual editing when code changes are actually required.

---

# Thin Controller Rule

API routes must remain extremely small.

API routes should only:

* receive requests
* validate input
* call a pipeline
* return responses

They should never contain:

* retry logic
* AI orchestration
* embedding logic
* search logic
* prompt construction
* model fallback logic

Business logic belongs inside `src/ai`.

---

# AI Pipeline Rule

Every AI workflow must eventually live under:

```text
src/ai/pipelines/
```

Examples:

```text
src/ai/pipelines/
    nehemiahPipeline.ts
    sermonPipeline.ts
    lessonPipeline.ts
    announcementPipeline.ts
```

Pipelines own:

* orchestration
* retries
* fallbacks
* telemetry
* logging
* AI workflow decisions

API routes simply invoke pipelines.

---

# Architecture Rules

Maintain the following structure:

```text
src/

    ai/
        embeddings/
        search/
        prompts/
        llm/
        pipelines/

    components/

    lib/

    data/
```

Keep modules:

* reusable
* testable
* independent

Avoid circular dependencies.

---

